TODO items for mcstas-ess-instr-citool
======================================

* runci: Consider running the tests of the different modes in parallel (as
  independent mctest runs, each with its own test directory), to make CI
  faster for projects with several modes. MPI (runci --mpi) does not help
  much for typical tests, since compilation with MPI takes longer, and the
  default mctest simulations are short.

* runci: mctest plots the output of each test (an overview PDF and HTML
  plots of all monitors), which nobody looks at in CI, and which can take much
  longer than the tests (about 14 of 15 minutes for the HEIMDAL model). The
  tool passes --noplots to mctest when mctest supports it (added in
  https://github.com/mccode-dev/McCode/pull/2734, not yet released). Once a
  McStas release with it is required, always pass it.

* runci: The JSON test results produced by mctest are only used to check for
  failures which mctest did not report in its exit code (see check_results
  in runtest.py). Decide whether to use them for anything else (e.g.
  summarise, store as CI artifacts, compare between runs).

* generatepy: The instrument renaming and the translation of %Example lines
  into McStasScript tests are done by the tool when mcstas-pygen does not
  support this (McStas 3.8.8). These features were added to mcstas-pygen in
  https://github.com/mccode-dev/McCode/pull/2707 (merged, not yet released).
  Once a McStas release with them is required, remove the workarounds in
  generatepy.py. The generated test code (like that of mcstas-pygen) sets and
  restores the instrument parameters around add_test calls. With McStasScript
  0.0.94 (now required), add_test(..., parameters={...}) could be used
  instead, also in the ExPyHelpers and ExPyExtra examples.

* %Example lines without any parameters fail in mctest (the instrument then
  asks for the parameter values interactively). This is fixed in mctest by
  https://github.com/mccode-dev/McCode/pull/2708 (merged, not yet released),
  which passes -y for such lines. Until a McStas release with this fix is
  required, such lines must list at least one parameter (as done by
  add_example in the ExPyHelpers example).

* localdata/: Consider a size limit (or a policy for large files, e.g. Git
  LFS) for data files. In the surveyed instrument repositories, the largest
  data file is a 38 MB .hkl file (MAGiC).

* McStasScript: instr.add_search(cmd, SHELL=True) writes a "SEARCH SHELL"
  statement, but also tries to load components from a folder named like the
  command itself (printing "Did not find specified folder"), so components
  found only via the command cannot be used with add_component. Propose a
  McStasScript PR which runs the command (as McStas does) and loads the
  components from the resulting directories. This is needed for the
  translation of SEARCH SHELL statements by mcstas-pygen (see next item).
  Any file lookup added in that PR must follow the locations searched by
  McStas's Open_File() (read_table-lib), which will no longer include $HOME
  (see the NCrystal search path item below). McStasScript itself does not
  currently search $HOME.

* mcstas-pygen drops SEARCH statements, so generatepy adds
  instr.add_search() for localcomps/ itself. A fix is proposed in
  https://github.com/mccode-dev/McCode/pull/2718 (merged, not yet
  released): mcstas-pygen
  translates SEARCH statements into instr.add_search() calls (relative paths
  relative to the generated script), and the generated make() uses the
  directory of the script as default input_path. Once a McStas release with it
  is required, remove the workaround in generatepy (and possibly the copying of
  localdata/ for the instrpy layout).

* NCrystal resolves relative paths of data files (e.g.
  "localdata/X.ncmat") only relative to the working directory, unlike McStas
  components using read_table-lib, which also look relative to the .instr
  file. This is fixed by https://github.com/mccode-dev/McCode/pull/2714
  (merged, not yet released): the NCrystal components, PowderN and
  Single_crystal add the same directories to the NCrystal search path as
  McStas's Open_File() searches. Once released, update the README
  (instruments using NCrystal then also work when not run from their own
  directory).

* NCrystal custom search directories (ncrystal_add_custom_search_dir, used
  by mccode_setup_ncrystal_search_paths in McCode):
  - Bug: NCrystal::DataSources::addCustomSearchDirectory asserts strictly
    decreasing priorities after sorting, but the C API always uses the same
    default priority, so adding two different directories violates the
    assertion (harmless in normal builds, where it is compiled out).
  - Custom directories have a lower priority than NCrystal's standard library,
    so a local file with the same name as a standard library file (e.g.
    Al_sg225.ncmat next to the .instr file) is shadowed by the standard
    library version (unless it is in the working directory), unlike McStas's
    Open_File(). Also, paths containing ".." are not accepted in custom
    directories.
  Perhaps an NCrystal JSON query could allow more flexible modifications,
  including of priorities. To be determined later; the McCode side
  (mccode_setup_ncrystal_search_paths) can then be updated accordingly.

* Components which read data files by fixed file names, like the McStas
  3.8.8 Union_abs_logger_nD_scintillator.comp (used by SKADI), which reads
  *_pixel_hit_efficiency*.txt tables, do not find them in localdata/ (McStas
  looks in the working directory, next to the .instr file and in the McStas
  data directories). Propose upstream that such components get a parameter
  for the location of the files.

* Instrument repositories at git.esss.dk/dmsc-instrumentmodels: TBL, FREIA
  and ODIN have been converted to the standard layout (standard-layout
  branches, with GitLab CI running runci). Convert the remaining ones.
